# is the whole point of them
# ==============================================================================
# REASON: third-party crawl capture; byte-fidelity is enforced by another gate.
# workshop/docs/the-platform/ledger/captures/*/body holds the VERBATIM response
# body of a page fetched from someone else's site. The first finding here was
# cap_c56a6cfeb46e — 302 KB of pi.dev's "RPC Mode" documentation — and its three
# "hardcoded paths" are HTML-escaped JSON inside PI.DEV'S OWN DOCS, showing their
# example slash-command definitions (session-name, fix-tests, skill:brave-search).
# They are not this project's paths and they are not configuration.
#
# EDITING A CAPTURE WOULD DO TWO KINDS OF DAMAGE, and the second is mechanical:
#   1. It falsifies the evidence. A capture exists so a citation can be replayed;
#      a capture we edited proves nothing about what the site actually served.
#   2. It BREAKS AN EXISTING GATE. verify_crawl.py:40 declares
#      "X10 REPLAY: every stored body re-hashes to the capture that names it".
#      Rewriting a byte makes that hash mismatch, so the honest fix here is
#      forbidden by another instrument.
#
# This is the same reasoning already accepted for the benchmark result files: a
# record of what was MEASURED must not be rewritten to look tidy, because the
# rewrite destroys the thing the record is for.
#
# SCOPE IS DELIBERATELY THE CAPTURE BODY AND NOTHING ELSE. The crawler's own
# code, its manifests and its metadata are NOT covered by this rule and remain
# fully audited — only the fetched payload is exempt.
#
# NOTE ON FORM, measured rather than assumed: this allow-list matches paths by
# EXACT STRING (`allow_kind()` uses awk `$2 == want`), so a glob such as
# `.../captures/*/body` matches NOTHING — a first attempt used one and the gate
# correctly stayed red. Each capture therefore needs its own row. That is a
# BETTER property than a glob would be: a new capture cannot inherit this
# pardon silently, and whoever adds one has to look at it.
